WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:35.000
Have you ever been conned? Or maybe you know someone who's fallen for a scam? Do you wonder why intelligent people join cults or give money to psychics or get sucked into Ponzi schemes? Have you ever gone to Las Vegas or Atlantic City and wondered what it might feel like to play poker for high stakes? Are the big winners lucky, skillful, or both? Do you feel like your brain is too cluttered with unnecessary facts? Would you like to know how to better organize your mind? Are you wondering what on earth these questions have to do with each other?
你曾经被骗过吗？或者你认识的人里有谁上过当吗？你是否好奇为什么聪明人会加入邪教、给算命先生送钱，或者陷入庞氏骗局？你有没有去过拉斯维加斯或大西洋城，想过在高额赌注下玩扑克是什么感觉？那些大赢家是靠运气、靠技术，还是两者兼有？你是否觉得自己的大脑被太多无用的事实塞满了？你想知道如何更好地整理自己的思维吗？你是否在纳闷这些问题之间到底有什么联系？

2
00:00:35.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Welcome to Speaking of Psychology, the flagship podcast of the American Psychological Association that explores the links between psychological science and everyday life. I'm Kim Mills. At APA, we often say that psychology is relevant to every aspect of life and that studying psychology can better equip you whatever path you choose. Our guest today is a living, walking, and writing example of that. Maria Konnikova has a PhD in psychology from Columbia University, but she works primarily as a journalist, except for the many months she spent studying and playing Texas Holden, eventually becoming an international poker champion and winning over $300,000 in tournament earnings.
欢迎收听《心理学谈话》（Speaking of Psychology），这是美国心理学会的旗舰播客，旨在探索心理科学与日常生活之间的联系。我是金·米尔斯（Kim Mills）。在美国心理学会，我们常说心理学与生活的方方面面息息相关，无论你选择什么道路，学习心理学都能让你更好地装备自己。我们今天的嘉宾就是这方面的活生生、会走路、会写作的典范。玛丽亚·康尼科娃（Maria Konnikova）拥有哥伦比亚大学心理学博士学位，但她主要以记者的身份工作，除了她花了好几个月时间学习和玩德州扑克，最终成为国际扑克冠军并赢得了超过30万美元的锦标赛奖金之外。

3
00:01:18.000 --> 00:02:04.000
She's also studied con artists from fortune tellers to the purveyors of the Nigerian print scam to three-card Monty players on the streets of New York, and she has applied her knowledge of psychology to understanding and explaining how to think like Sherlock Holmes and how not to think like Dr. Watson. Konnikova has written three best-selling books, Mastermind How to Think Like Sherlock Holmes, the Confidence Game, and her latest, the biggest bluff. Her writing has been featured in Best American Science and Nature writing and has been translated into more than 20 languages. She's also a contributing writer for the New Yorker magazine and host of the podcast series The Grift, which explores, con artists and the lives they wreck. Maria Konnikova, welcome to speaking of psychology. Thank you so much for having me.
她还研究过各种骗子，从算命先生到尼日利亚骗局的始作俑者，再到纽约街头的“三张牌”赌徒。她运用自己的心理学知识来理解和解释如何像夏洛克·福尔摩斯那样思考，以及如何避免像华生医生那样思考。康尼科娃撰写了三本畅销书：《大师思维：像福尔摩斯一样思考》（Mastermind: How to Think Like Sherlock Holmes）、《欺骗游戏》（The Confidence Game），以及她最新的作品《最大的虚张声势》（The Biggest Bluff）。她的作品曾入选《美国最佳科学与自然写作》，并被翻译成20多种语言。她还是《纽约客》杂志的特约撰稿人，也是播客系列《骗局》（The Grift）的主持人，该系列探讨了骗子及其摧毁的人生。玛丽亚·康尼科娃，欢迎来到《心理学谈话》。非常感谢邀请我。

4
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:34.000
It's a pleasure. Just reading your biography could make anyone feel like a goldbricker, which is actually a type of work-of-wording grifter that you describe in the confidence game. And I didn't even mention that you did your graduate work at Harvard, where you studied psychology, creative writing, and government. Were you just trying to make up your mind what to be when you grew up? No, I think I always knew I wanted to be a writer. According to, well, I don't know if it's apocryphal, but I don't remember it because I was five years old or so.
这是我的荣幸。光是读你的简历就足以让人觉得自己像个“金砖骗子”（goldbricker），这实际上是你《欺骗游戏》中描述的一种骗子。我甚至还没提到你在哈佛大学完成了研究生学业，在那里你学习了心理学、创意写作和政府学。你当时是在纠结长大后要做什么吗？不，我想我一直都知道自己想成为一名作家。根据——好吧，我不知道这是否是杜撰的，因为我不记得了，因为那时我大概五岁左右。

5
00:02:34.000 --> 00:03:12.000
But according to my family, I informed everyone over dinner when I was about five that I was going to be a writer when I grew up. And that's all I've ever wanted to do. But I just love learning and I love the human mind. And so I didn't know that I loved psychology until high school. And we had this amazing psychology teacher, Pamela Lynn. So if she happens to listen to this, she's wonderful. And she taught advanced placement psychology. And she assigned Oliver Sacks, the man who mistook his wife for a hat as summer reading.
但据我的家人说，我五岁左右在晚餐时告诉大家，我长大后要成为一名作家。这就是我一直想做的事情。但我只是热爱学习，热爱人类的心智。所以我直到高中才知道自己热爱心理学。我们有一位非常棒的心理学老师，帕梅拉·林恩（Pamela Lynn）。如果她碰巧在听这个节目，她真的很棒。她教的是大学先修课程心理学。她布置的暑期阅读书目是奥利弗·萨克斯（Oliver Sacks）的《错把妻子当帽子的人》（The Man Who Mistook His Wife for a Hat）。

6
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:43.000
And I remember reading that and just saying, oh, my God, my mind has been blown. You can do this. You can write about this. This is ridiculously cool. And we also read Stephen Pinker's The Language Instinct. You know, she assigned, I think, some of the greats who both practiced and were brilliant writers. So when I, by the time I got to college, I thought, oh, I'm going to register. So as you mentioned, I did my undergrad at Harvard.
我记得读完那本书后，我惊叹道：“天哪，我的大脑被震撼了。”你可以做这个。你可以写关于这个的内容。这简直太酷了。我们还读了斯蒂芬·平克（Stephen Pinker）的《语言本能》（The Language Instinct）。你知道，她布置的阅读书目里，我想，都是一些既是实践者又是杰出作家的伟人。所以，当我上大学时，我想，哦，我要注册。正如你提到的，我在哈佛读了本科。

7
00:03:43.000 --> 00:04:09.000
And I thought, oh, I'm going to register at MIT so that I could take a class with Stephen Pinker. But luckily for me, he came to Harvard. so I didn't have to do a cross-registration at MIT, and he ended up actually becoming my undergrad advisor for my thesis, which was in government and psychology, but I also did creative writing, and I did fiction because I had thought that I was going to be a novelist when I grew up, which did not end up happening, but I still love fiction,
我当时想，我要去麻省理工学院（MIT）注册，这样我就能上斯蒂芬·平克的课。但幸运的是，他来到了哈佛，所以我不需要去麻省理工学院跨校注册。他最终成为了我本科论文的导师，我的论文方向是政府学和心理学，但我还选修了创意写作，写的是小说，因为我曾以为自己长大后会成为一名小说家，虽然最终没能实现，但我仍然热爱小说，

8
00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:43.000
and I love reading fiction, and I hope one day, you know, I have probably like a lot of writers, I have, you know, the novel in the drawer, and I hope to one day have the time to explore that side more freely. But this was a very long way to say, I always wanted to be a writer, but I love people and how minds work and how they think. That's what excites me about writing. It's what excites me about psychology. It's why I wanted to be a fiction writer, because as I've said before in other interviews,
我热爱阅读小说，我希望有一天，你知道，可能像许多作家一样，我有一个“抽屉里的小说”，我希望有一天能有时间更自由地探索那一面。但这只是为了说明，我一直想成为一名作家，但我热爱人类，热爱心智如何运作以及人们如何思考。这就是写作让我兴奋的地方。这也是心理学让我兴奋的地方。这就是我为什么想成为一名小说家，因为正如我在其他采访中所说的那样，

9
00:04:43.000 --> 00:05:38.000
I think that the best psychologists are the great novelists and the great poets because they, have had to have such great insight into the human condition to write the way they do and to observe the way they do, that oftentimes they reach certain insights that experimental psychology ends up proving, you know, 100 years later. So in your most recent book, the biggest bluff, you detail how you became a professional poker player. What made you decide to learn to play Texas Haldem? I understand you didn't even have a deck of cards in your house when you were a kid. So why that game and not say another form of poker or even chess? That is all because of the great John von Neumann, who was one of the great polymaths of the 20th century, the father of game theory, the father of the computer, one of the fathers of the hydrogen bomb, just an all-around genius.
我认为最好的心理学家是伟大的小说家和伟大的诗人，因为他们必须对人类状况有如此深刻的洞察力，才能写出那样的作品，才能进行那样的观察。以至于他们往往能得出某些洞察，而实验心理学最终在100年后才证明了这些洞察。那么，在你最近的书《最大的虚张声势》中，你详细描述了自己是如何成为一名职业扑克选手的。是什么让你决定学习玩德州扑克？我听说你小时候家里甚至连一副扑克牌都没有。所以为什么是那个游戏，而不是其他形式的扑克，甚至是国际象棋？这完全是因为伟大的约翰·冯·诺依曼（John von Neumann），他是20世纪伟大的博学家之一，博弈论之父，计算机之父，氢弹之父之一，简直是一位全能的天才。

10
00:05:38.000 --> 00:06:30.000
And in his kind of the foundational text of game theory, the theory of games and economic behavior, which he co-wrote, he writes about games and talks about the fact that he was looking for a way to model complex strategic decision-making, human decision-making at the highest levels. Now, at this time, he was also advising the Security Council of the United States. He was talking about all of the big security issues of the day. So this was not merely academic. And it turns out that game theory is based on poker and that he dismissed chess as actually being completely boring and irrelevant for human decision making because it's a game of perfect information. It's a game where you see everything, right?
在他那本博弈论的奠基性著作《博弈论与经济行为》（Theory of Games and Economic Behavior）中，他与人合著了这本书，他写到了游戏，并谈到了他正在寻找一种方法来模拟复杂的战略决策，即最高水平的人类决策。当时，他还在为美国国家安全委员会提供咨询。他谈论的是当时所有重大的安全问题。所以这不仅仅是学术性的。事实证明，博弈论是以扑克为基础的，他认为国际象棋对于人类决策来说是完全无聊且无关紧要的，因为它是一个“完美信息”游戏。这是一个你能看到一切的游戏，对吧？

11
00:06:30.000 --> 00:07:17.000
You see the entire board. You see all the pieces. And theoretically with enough computing power, you can solve it. there's always a right move. Whereas if you look at a game like poker, that's much more true to life because it's a game of incomplete information. It's a game of uncertainty. It's a game of unknowns. It's a game of people. As he wrote, and this is a quote from him, real life consists of bluffing, of trying to figure out what does this man think I mean to do. And that is what games are about in my theory. And if you think about it, that's just this beautiful insight into psychology and into the fact that here's this mathematician who's saying math is not enough.
你能看到整个棋盘。你能看到所有的棋子。理论上，只要有足够的计算能力，你就能解开它。总有一个正确的走法。而如果你看像扑克这样的游戏，它更贴近生活，因为它是一个“不完全信息”游戏。这是一个充满不确定性的游戏。这是一个充满未知数的游戏。这是一个关于人的游戏。正如他所写的——这是引用他的话——“现实生活包括虚张声势，试图弄清楚这个人认为我打算做什么。这就是我的理论中游戏的核心。”如果你仔细想想，这对于心理学来说是一个非常美丽的洞察，而且这位数学家说数学是不够的。

12
00:07:17.000 --> 00:08:37.000
You know, computation is not enough. Chess is not life because life is like poker. Life is a game of unknowns of incomplete information where you have to make all of these deductions and you have to constantly change and adjust based on the people you're playing with. And so math is not enough. You need psychology. And that's where game theory kind of came in. He wanted to solve poker. And he thought that if he could solve it, he would have the answer the key to strategic decision making that he'd be able to prevent nuclear war. Really, that's not me exaggerating. And no limit holdum is the variation of poker that in terms of the balance of known to unknown information, best mirrors decision-making in life. Because in some other variations, there's too much unknown, right? There are too many down cards and too much private information, and it becomes too much gambling, right? It becomes too much like roulette. There's not quite enough to go on. And in other variants of poker, there's too much information in common, and it becomes too kind of easy to solve because there's only one whole card and everything else is visible and all the other information is in common. And so no limit holdum is kind of this beautiful balance of knowns to
你知道，计算是不够的。国际象棋不是生活，因为生活就像扑克。生活是一个充满未知数、充满不完全信息的游戏，你必须做出所有这些推论，并且必须根据你正在与之博弈的人不断改变和调整。所以数学是不够的。你需要心理学。这就是博弈论的切入点。他想解开扑克。他认为如果他能解开它，他就能找到战略决策的关键，从而能够防止核战争。真的，我没有夸张。而无限注德州扑克（No-limit Hold'em）是扑克的一种变体，在已知信息与未知信息的平衡方面，最能反映生活中的决策。因为在其他一些变体中，未知信息太多了，对吧？有太多的底牌和太多的私人信息，它变得太像赌博了，对吧？它变得太像轮盘赌了。没有足够的信息可供参考。而在其他扑克变体中，公共信息太多了，它变得太容易解开了，因为只有一张底牌，其他一切都是可见的，所有其他信息都是公共的。所以无限注德州扑克是一种已知与

13
00:08:37.000 --> 00:09:25.000
unknown. And obviously limit holden is exactly the same in the sense of knowns to unknown. But if you're talking about the game of life, it's a game of no limit. It's a game where you can always wager anything at any given point in time. So it's a much better analog. So I, when I read about this, as you said, I didn't even have a deck of cards. I didn't know. how many cards were in a deck. I had zero interest in any of this, but it intrigued me. And so I started reading about poker, and I just thought, this is, you know, this is it. Why don't I learn to play this game and use it as a way of exploring the balance of skill versus chance in life and to kind of learn to see what kind of a decision maker I can become in this new world that I know
未知的完美平衡。显然，有限注德州扑克在已知与未知的意义上是完全一样的。但如果你谈论的是生活这场游戏，它是一场无限注的游戏。这是一个你可以在任何给定时间点下注任何东西的游戏。所以它是一个更好的模拟。所以，当我读到这些时，正如你所说，我甚至连一副牌都没有。我不知道一副牌里有多少张牌。我对这一切毫无兴趣，但它引起了我的好奇。于是我开始阅读关于扑克的内容，我只是想，这就是了。为什么我不学习玩这个游戏，并把它作为一种探索生活中技能与运气平衡的方式，并学习看看在这个我一无所知的全新世界里，我能成为什么样的决策者呢？

